<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1895 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">America the Beautiful</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song from the 1890s that praises the land of the United States, from its fields to its mountains, sea to sea.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Katharine Lee Bates</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Samuel A. Ward</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the United States</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Colorado Springs, Colorado (the words); Newark, New Jersey (the music)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem written 1893, published 4 July 1895; tune 1882; first published together 1910</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Katharine Lee Bates (published 1895, revised 1904; she died in 1929); music by Samuel A. Ward (1882; he died in 1903))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>In 1893 Katharine Lee Bates, a 33-year-old English professor at Wellesley College, took the train to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> Springs to teach at Colorado College. Sights on the journey found their way into her poem: the World's Columbian Exposition in Chicago, the &quot;White City&quot; whose gleaming buildings promised a future; the wheat fields of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>, through which her train passed on 16 July; and the view of the Great Plains from the top of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">Pikes</a> Peak. The words began to come to her on the summit, and she wrote them down back in her room at the Antlers Hotel. The poem, first called &quot;Pikes Peak&quot;, was published as &quot;America&quot; in the Fourth of July 1895 issue of the church paper The Congregationalist. It quickly caught the public's fancy, and an amended version appeared in 1904.</p><p>The music came separately. Samuel A. Ward, organist and choirmaster of Grace Episcopal Church in Newark, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, had written a hymn tune in 1882; it came to him on a ferry from Coney Island back to his home after a summer day, and he wrote it down at once. He set it to the old hymn &quot;O Mother Dear, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>&quot; and named it &quot;Materna&quot;, and the hymn was published in 1892. Many tunes were tried for Bates's poem, the first sent in by Silas Pratt and at least 75 by 1900, but Ward's was thought the best, and the words and his tune were first published together, as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/america-the-beautiful">America the Beautiful</a>&quot;, in 1910. The two never met. Ward died in 1903, not knowing what his music would become; by Bates's death in 1929 the song's popularity was well established, and it appears in the songbooks of many American congregations.</p><p>At various times, above all during the presidency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a>, there have been efforts to make it a national hymn or anthem beside or in place of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-star-spangled-banner">The Star-Spangled Banner</a>&quot;, and before 1931 it was considered a candidate for the national anthem. Supporters say it is easier to sing and more melodic, and some object to the war imagery of the anthem and to its third verse's implied support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and racism; others prefer the anthem for its war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a>. None of these efforts has succeeded.</p><p>Ray Charles's soulful version reached the R&amp;B chart in the Bicentennial year of 1976 and is traditionally played on New Year's Eve after the Times Square Ball drop; he sang it at the 1984 Republican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> and at Super Bowl XXXV in 2001, and Whitney Houston covered his arrangement in 1991. Its popularity grew in the years after the September 11 attacks, when it was sung at sporting events beside the national anthem, and it has opened many Super Bowls and editions of WrestleMania. A multilingual version in a Coca-Cola advertisement for Super Bowl XLVIII drew criticism from some conservatives. At the 2023 US Open, Cécile McLorin Salvant sang, in place of the familiar lines of the first verse, the second verse's prayer that God mend the nation's every flaw, a choice the jazz critic Nate Chinen found full of meaning. Jennifer Lopez sang it at the inauguration of Joe Biden in 2021 and Carrie Underwood at the second inauguration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donald-trump">Donald Trump</a> in 2025. Its phrases &quot;from sea to shining sea&quot; and &quot;purple mountain majesties&quot;, the shade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">Pikes</a> Peak, have become American idioms.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of praise to the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, from its skies and grain fields to its mountains and plains, joined to a prayer that God bless the country, correct its faults and make its people worthy of it. The mood is reverent and hopeful rather than martial.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O beautiful for spacious skies,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For amber waves of grain,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For purple mountain majesties</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Above the fruited plain!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>America! America!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God shed His grace on thee,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And crown thy good with brotherhood</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>From sea to shining sea!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O beautiful for pilgrim feet</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Whose stern impassion'd stress</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A thoroughfare for freedom beat</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Across the wilderness.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>America! America!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God mend thine ev'ry flaw,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Confirm thy soul in self-control,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Thy liberty in law.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O beautiful for heroes prov'd</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In liberating strife,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Who more than self their country loved,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And mercy more than life.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>America! America!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>May God thy gold refine</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Till all success be nobleness,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And ev'ry gain divine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O beautiful for patriot dream</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That sees beyond the years</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Thine alabaster cities gleam</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Undimmed by human tears.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>America! America!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God shed His grace on thee,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And crown thy good with brotherhood</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>From sea to shining sea.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Katharine Lee Bates; from the sheet music published 1917. Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/America_the_Beautiful_(1917)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="2FXN1Z6Q004" data-title="America the Beautiful: Ray Charles, official audio of his soul version (charted 1976), his own arrangement"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Ray Charles, official audio of his soul version (charted 1976), his own arrangement</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Ray Charles. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2FXN1Z6Q004" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">America the Beautiful (Ray Charles)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1976</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Whitney Houston (B-side of The Star Spangled Banner)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1991</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording of Ray Charles's arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Elvis Presley (B-side of My Way)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1977</span><span class="sg-chain-h">live recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Frank Sinatra with Nelson Riddle</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1963</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording (issued 2012)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Amber Waves (Tori Amos)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2003</span><span class="sg-chain-h">phrase borrowed</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Shining Sea Bikeway</span><span class="sg-chain-h">named after the phrase</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Materna (Samuel A. Ward), tune for &quot;O Mother Dear, Jerusalem&quot;</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1882</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pikes Peak (Katharine Lee Bates)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1893</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the poem's first title</span></li></ul></section>
